--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e9620f8c34305754d8cc9a7e49e852d9, type: 3}
  m_Name: Chapter 6 text_en
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Localization::UnityEngine.Localization.Tables.StringTable
  m_LocaleId:
    m_Code: en
  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 358719196651e7c4aaaabf8f7677e331, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 452740272128
    m_Localized: And now here all <color=#48DBFB>4 inputs</color> are already in
      use!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 489381711872
    m_Localized: We set <color=yellow>addresses</color> (numbers from <color=#48DBFB>0
      to 15</color>) to read out the values of <color=#48DBFB>Registers 2 and 8</color>,
      and at the same time we read out address <color=yellow>A3</color>. This address
      determines <anim:shake><color=orange>WHICH REGISTER</color></anim> the new
      data gets written to.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 502178533376
    m_Localized: "This <color=#48DBFB>\u201Enew data\u201C</color> arrives at input
      <color=yellow>WD3</color>. During the write operation, close attention is then
      paid to which address is currently present at <color=yellow>A3</color>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 509602451456
    m_Localized: If anything's unclear - just call me.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 545363087360
    m_Localized: <anim:shake><color=red>BEHOLD IT!</color></anim> Your last stage
      before the <color=orange>multi-cycle processor</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 579022376960
    m_Localized: Remember what I explained to you (or what you <anim:wave>skipped</anim>...)
      and decide which value you pass on to the <color=orange>Multiplexer</color>
      next. Think about which <color=yellow>type</color> our instruction is and which
      <color=yellow>operands</color> are needed.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 592452538368
    m_Localized: If you really want to make life hard for yourself, try not to overwrite
      <color=red>any unnecessary data</color>, but only the <color=green>target register</color>
      (and specifically in exactly the <color=#48DBFB>clock cycle</color> where that's
      actually supposed to happen!).
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3889469083648
    m_Localized: 'And there it is: the last building block of our processor - the
      <color=orange>RegisterFile</color>!'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3930296438784
    m_Localized: 'Here all <color=#48DBFB>16 registers</color> are gathered (from
      the <color=#48DBFB>0th to the 15th</color>). The name of a register, for example
      <color=#48DBFB>r0</color>, doesn''t just stand for its <color=#48DBFB>number</color>,
      but also directly for its <color=yellow>address</color>.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3939742011392
    m_Localized: 'What do I mean by that? It''s simple: if we want to read a value
      from register <color=#48DBFB>r0</color> or write one into it, we have to specify
      its address at the input - in this case that''s <color=#48DBFB>0</color>.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3948633935872
    m_Localized: So we need to transmit numbers from <color=#48DBFB>0 to 15</color>
      to access one specific register in the <color=orange>RegisterFile</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3957806878720
    m_Localized: "Speaking of \u201Etransmit\u201C... Do you actually know how many
      inputs the <color=orange>RegisterFile</color> has and what they're good for?"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3966740746240
    m_Localized: <anim:wave>Well, greeeat!</anim> Then let's get started with the
      second-to-last chapter!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3975032885248
    m_Localized: 'You''ve got a point there too. But let''s take a quick look at
      them anyway: there are a total of <color=#48DBFB>4 inputs</color> and <color=#48DBFB>2
      outputs</color>.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4714727755776
    m_Localized: "The inputs <color=yellow>A1</color> and <color=yellow>A2</color>
      take in the <color=yellow>register addresses</color> from the instruction (Command).
      That means for <color=#48DBFB>\u201Eadd r0, r1, r2\u201C</color> it would be
      <color=yellow>A1</color> = <color=#48DBFB>1</color> and <color=yellow>A2</color>
      = <color=#48DBFB>2</color>."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4725737803776
    m_Localized: The input <color=yellow>A3</color> tells us which address the result
      of the calculation should be written to. So here too a <color=yellow>register
      address</color> from <color=#48DBFB>0 to 15</color> is passed in.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4736609439744
    m_Localized: <color=yellow>WD3</color> then transmits the actual <color=green>value</color>
      that gets stored at address <color=yellow>A3</color>. This value was usually
      calculated by the <color=orange>ALU</color> beforehand.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4745279066112
    m_Localized: 'And what about the <color=yellow>outputs</color>? They simply output
      the values of the registers located at addresses <color=yellow>A1</color> and
      <color=yellow>A2</color>.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4754682695680
    m_Localized: What happens if there's no second register, meaning address A2 doesn't
      exist, or we don't need the value? Then we usually ignore the second output
      with a <color=orange>Multiplexer</color> (that doesn't exist in this task yet,
      but you'll see it later).
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4763658506240
    m_Localized: In this level and the next one we only use the first two inputs
      - <color=yellow>A1</color> and <color=yellow>A2</color>. That means we simply
      read out the <color=yellow>register addresses</color> and fetch their <color=#48DBFB>values</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 66710772928512
    m_Localized: Sure, there aren't that many.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 66822416912384
    m_Localized: Who's going to bother counting those?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 80043882336256
    m_Localized: 'Think carefully about which <color=yellow>inputs</color> are responsible
      for what:



      The <color=#48DBFB>top two</color> accept the <color=yellow>register
      number</color>.

      The <color=#48DBFB>third</color> accepts the <color=yellow>register
      address</color> that is to be <color=green>overwritten</color> with the value
      from the fourth input.

      The <color=#48DBFB>fourth</color> accepts the
      <color=yellow>value</color> that gets stored at the address of the third input.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 80086148337664
    m_Localized: 'What exactly is the instruction from <color=yellow>Register A2</color>
      supposed to do?

      How many <color=yellow>operands</color> does it have,
      and which ones?

      How many <color=yellow>clock cycles</color> are needed
      to fully process this instruction?'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2188748664885248
    m_Localized: Write the sum of r7 and r6 into Register 3.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2188793124507648
    m_Localized: Use the register values to calculate the sum. Write this sum to
      the address of A3
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2188803023065088
    m_Localized: Process the instruction from Register A2 correctly and write the
      result into RegisterFile
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
